From 61423f4a237c2d5337a6739e4e83befa0512076a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ebg-ant <296364998+ebg-ant@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 15:05:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix: one word for one key: it fills the screen; and two lines
 say what strangers can follow
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The welcome says that ⌃⌥↩ fills the screen with a window. The line after a dock said "⌃⌥↩ frames it", and the menu's row for the
same key read "Frame Focused Window": whoever had learned the one did not find the other. The glossary decided for "fill the
screen" on 2026-10-15, by twelve fresh readers, and ended "the strings follow; until then the app still says Frame".

These are the strings of the key: the menu's row, and with it the panel of shortcuts and Go to…, reads Fill the Screen; the
welcome, where the key is unbound, names the row by its new words. The same command for a window or a space that is named is
called likewise. The pages that teach the key follow (README, KEYBOARD, GUIDE, MENU). No command, key, setting or reply changes.

Two lines, by the product owner's word after three strangers had read stills of them. The line after a dock loses "Docked",
which none of them understood: "⌃⌥↩ fills the screen with it" says what the key does, not what Arbor calls the state; where no
key can be named it reads "Fill the screen with it from Arbor’s menu". And when that key has taken the other windows out of view
the line teaches one way back, the key that did it: "⌃⌥↩ again goes back", where it named ⌃⌥Space ("which one is it?"). Only after
the key's own command: by another road (a click on a tile, Step In, a window gone to) nobody has pressed it, and with the view on
a group it does not lead back at all. There the line keeps "⌃⌥Space shows everything", which is true from anywhere.

That way back is one only while the window that had the keyboard has it: in another's hands the key fills the screen with that
one. What is on screen is never untrue, so the line goes when the keyboard goes elsewhere.

A display is never a screen, says the glossary, and two tests hold titles to that. "Fill the screen" is the glossary's own
phrase: it says so now beside "screen edge", and the tests follow.

Replies still say "framed", and so do the help of the setting `shortcuts`, `help`, and the words of another gesture (Frame a
Rectangle, Frame What Is Inside) and of a setting (New windows while framed). Go to… finds a command by the menu's name alone: by
"fill" and "screen" now, no longer by "frame", "window" or "focused".
